---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";
---

<Base title="About" description="About Zach Koncir and where the name one24 comes from.">
	<div class="wrap">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>
				I’m {SITE.name}, a principal engineer with more than 15 years in infrastructure. I came up through
				network operations and DevOps, led an AWS consulting practice, and now work on a platform that runs some of
				the world’s most-played online games. Lately my work centres on one question: how do engineers stay in
				charge as AI agents take on more of the work?
			</p>

			<h2>How I work</h2>
			<p>
				I care about the unglamorous parts of infrastructure: clear interfaces, safe defaults, and making the
				right thing the easy thing for the engineers who build on what I make.
			</p>
			<p>
				The same idea has run through my work for years, from “freedom within boundaries” at DevOpsDays in 2019
				to the guardrails in pi-containment today: give engineers room to move fast, and put the limits where they
				protect everyone. AI agents haven’t changed that. They’ve raised the stakes. An agent is just another user
				of your platform, and it deserves the same well-paved paths and the same firm boundaries.
			</p>

			<h2>Why one24</h2>
			<p>
				For most of my life I raced motocross, and 124 was the number on my plate. Years later the number
				came back: my first daughter was born on December 4, 12/4. So one24 is two things I care about, neither
				of which has anything to do with software, which is sort of the point.
			</p>

			<h2>Get in touch</h2>
			<p>
				The best way to reach me is email: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. You can also find
				me on <a href={SITE.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>, see my code on <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a>, or read my{" "}
				<a href="/cv/">CV</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	h1 {
		font-size: var(--step-4);
		margin: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem) 0 2rem;
	}
</style>
